EXPLAINATION:-
In my Blood Donor Management System, the DonorController acts as the entry point for donor-related REST API requests.
When a donor registers, the controller receives the JSON request and converts it into a Donor object. 
It calls the GeocodingService to obtain latitude and longitude from the donor's address. 
Then, it saves the donor through the DonorRepository, retrieves the associated hospital, and calls the InventoryService to update the blood stock.
When a donation is recorded, the controller calls the BadgeService. 
This service saves the donation history, updates the donor's donation count and last donation date, and calculates the badge level. 
The InventoryService is then called to update the corresponding blood group stock.
Finally, the controller returns the saved donor or donation details as a JSON response to the frontend. I use Spring Data JPA repositories to interact with the MySQL database.
